TH the table row and the prose: 'In the large-A limit, (1-h^2)^n -> 1 since h^2 approx 0.044 << 1, recovering the standard BW formula for heavy nuclei', and table row 'A >= 5 | many | (1-h^2)^n -> 1 | 1'. Recomputed at the proton pin q_0 = 1.1099785827513808: h = q - 1/q = 0.2090602988, h^2 = 0.04370620853, so 1 - h^2 = 0.9562937915 and (1-h^2)^n DECAYS geometrically to 0, not to 1 \u2014 0.7998 at n=5, 0.6396 at n=10, 0.1070 at n=50, 0.01146 at n=100. Because 0 < h^2 < 1, the limit is 0 for every h != 0; 'h^2 << 1' controls the RATE, not the limit. The consequence is not cosmetic: a(N) -> 0 multiplies the Bethe-Weizsaecker binding to zero for heavy nuclei, i.e. the stated limit is what would recover BW, and the actual limit destroys it. The block's own verified rows are fine (1+h = 1.20906 -> 1.209; 1-h^2 = 0.95629 -> 0.956; (1-h^2)^2 = 0.91450 -> 0.914; h^2 -> 0.044 \u2014 all reproduce at either the proton or the muon pin, which agree to 8 digits here). Proposed fix: either n must be bounded (state the bound and drop 'many'/'large-A'), or the per-pair factor must be (1 - h^2/n)-like / normalised so the product converges; as written the A >= 5 row and the closing sentence contradict the formula displayed six lines above them.","reviewer":"agent","by":"agent:v03","model":null,"at":"2026-08-21","stale":false,"history":2},{"id":"trap-performed-warmth","group":"Voice","result":"pass","severity":null,"notes":"","reviewer":"agent","by":"agent:v03","model":null,"at":"2026-08-21","stale":false,"history":2},{"id":"trap-rhetorical-pivot","group":"Voice","result":"pass","severity":null,"notes":"","reviewer":"agent","by":"agent:v03","model":null,"at":"2026-08-21","stale":false,"history":2},{"id":"trap-superlative","group":"Voice","result":"pass","severity":null,"notes":"","reviewer":"agent","by":"agent:v03","model":null,"at":"2026-08-21","stale":false,"history":2},{"id":"trap-thesis-restatement","group":"Voice","result":"pass","severity":null,"notes":"","reviewer":"agent","by